{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of S6 at head 4b9f6e8907, tree 3e53fd51, for request ddf2a597 and review request d936527d. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head adds 37 paths to main 09420198. I read the note and the core MCP diff, and an independent read went through the whole diff, with small scratch runs on `diff.ts` outside the repository. I confirmed items 1, 2 and 3 and items 4 to 7's code in the source. The scratch results and the esbuild reach check below come from that read, and I did not repeat them.\n\nThe version binding holds, and that was the hard part. The defects are in what a card offers around an unknown outcome and in how `diff.ts` handles hostile names.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. A card locks for good on an act that never arrived, and the app has no way out.\n   - A card's act that fails in transport before reaching the scope stays `unknown` in custody. `#checkOriginal` then replaces every subject card's acts at that scope with Check original. `settle` finds no receipt and keeps the record, so Approve and Merge never return.\n   - `createRepoInAiService` sets no mode, so `act` is refused, and \"call the same tool again\" names a tool that service doesn't have. The only exit is a separate `--generic` service over the same home, with the exact kind, `on`, fields and `at`, and the display strips the fields.\n   - This is your point 3, and my answer is no. Fail-closed is right while the act could still land, but a permanent stop with no route isn't a limit that the owner can act on.\n   - Fix: give it an exit. One way: once the signed intent's `notAfter` has passed and the scope has no record of it, the act can never land, so the record can resolve as not sent and the card can read fresh. State the route you choose, witness it, and bring it to the planner if it changes custody's meaning.\n\n2. Check original on a merge held elsewhere tells the caller to sign.\n   - `service.ts:995` answers `not-found` with \"Nothing was sent under this key from this server; sign with it.\"\n   - With the same key on another custody home and the merge unknown, the card offers the check, and that answer comes back while the merge exists. An agent reading it is told to sign.\n   - Witness 2's comment steers around this press.\n   - Fix: say only that this server keeps no record, and that this doesn't show the act wasn't sent. Add a case.\n\n3. A retained card can sign a merge while one is unknown.\n   - `#dispatch` makes no custody check, and `admit` returns early outside `decision`. Witness 2's `press(stale, ...)` shows the signature and the send, and the scope refuses it.\n   - If the first merge never arrived, no merge item exists, so no guard fires, and another retained card signs a second merge under a new key. The first envelope then fails on its revisions, so a double landing looks blocked, but this contradicts \"no card offers a merge\".\n   - Fix: run the same unresolved check at dispatch, before signing, for any card act other than the check itself. Add the never-arrived case. Keep witness 2's scope-refusal assertion through a path that doesn't depend on a retained card, or keep it by holding the check for that case only.\n\n4. A long path breaks the 40 KiB text bound (`diff.ts:108,123,204`).\n   - `why` embeds the unbounded path, and the closing line skips `#emit`. The scratch run gave 60,102 JSON bytes for a 60 KB name and 90,102 for 90 KB.\n   - Over 64 KiB, by reading, `checked` throws in `#readDiff` after the credential is spent, and that error omits \"spent\".\n   - Fix: bound `shown` everywhere, and include the closing line in the budget.\n\n5. A huge entry name throws instead of truncating (`diff.ts:48,62`).\n   - `String.fromCharCode(...name)` spreads the bytes, and a 500 KB name gave `RangeError` in the scratch run. The caller then reports \"the host gave no readable answer\", although the repository caused it, and the answer isn't `truncated`.\n   - Fix: don't spread, or refuse an oversized name as a bound, with `truncated` set.\n\n6. `shownPath` and file text pass author-controlled controls to a model and a card (`diff.ts:44,50,52-54`).\n   - U+009B and U+202E pass through raw, and a literal `a\\x0ab` renders the same as a name with a real newline. File content keeps ESC and CR.\n   - This is your point 5. Paths and text are data, but a reader must be able to trust what a path line says.\n   - Fix: escape C1 controls and bidi controls in paths, escape the backslash so the form is unambiguous, and state what file text keeps. Add a case.\n\n7. A refusal that could be known first spends the credential.\n   - `COMMIT_ID` admits 64 hex characters, `#diff` spends the credential, and then `objectId` refuses with `unsupported-object-format`.\n   - Fix: refuse before the credential if the reader can't take that format, or say `spent: true` on that path.\n\nItems 1 to 7 change production code, so one new full gate must run at the final source.\n\n## Your points\n\n1. Yes, that's enough for `capturedExpected`. `judge.ts:299-302` requires exact keys and then compares revisions, so an extra `on` or `also` gets `bad-intent`, a wrong value gets `revision-moved`, and an omission fails locally or at the scope. A card's `at` is server-held, and `view_act` takes only an ID. The comment's \"must be one this act binds\" is loose for `on`, so correct it to say that the scope decides.\n\n2. It's fair as a limit owned by the hosted witness. Don't call the Change view finished for a reviewer until that witness runs. If a destination token can't read an integration commit, View diff spends a credential to say unavailable exactly when a reviewer needs it. That result would change this design, so the planner should expect it.\n\n3. No. See item 1.\n\n4. Yes, witness 2's exact-name assertion carries it. That name can only come from that guard, and the case asserts the item state `unknown` first. C9 stays not evidence, as the note says.\n\n5. Mostly. Redirects are manual, non-200 answers are refused, `remoteUrl` requires HTTPS with no userinfo, `parseTree` refuses duplicates, ordering faults, slashes and dot names, and no credential appears in answers. Items 4 to 7 are the exceptions.\n\n6. Sound. The independent read built `apps/room/worker.ts`, `worker.commons.ts`, `worker.grow.ts` and `packages/scope/src/worker.ts` with esbuild metafiles, and they hold no `diff.ts`, `mcp/src/node.ts`, `apps/views` or `cli/src`.\n\n## What holds\n\n- Witnesses 1 to 4 assert what they claim. An old card fails with `revision-moved` and carries the displayed revisions. The lost-reply stand-in records the act, then holds the reply, and SIGKILL goes to the exact child PID. Witness 5 ran on stand-ins, as the request allows, and the note says so.\n- The custody check excludes `prepared` and standalone records, other signers, services and scopes, and `sent` is written before delivery.\n- `diff` is in `HOSTED_TOOL_OMISSIONS`, `readDiff` is checked before any spend or signature, and `#credential` requires this key's own read-token entry. The remote comes from the route.\n- `change.ts` filters reviews and jobs to this manifest, takes reviewer kinds from membership, labels agents with their controller, and reads only fixed names.\n- The seals change only in `hosted-tools.ts` and `service.ts`, which changed. `security-entry-points` and `active-source` pass 22 of 22.\n- The gate at 98c055ae exits 0. Its `test.log` shows Node 98, Counting 56, Plain 3,213 with one skip and Native 896 with two skips, and a stopped first gate stays in its own directory. From there to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- The evidence verifies. All 36 manifest rows match, the manifest has digest e1c5c1c4, the directory has 70 files, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 7a16d103, and no file is writable.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 09420198 gives tree 3e53fd51. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-commit-messages` checks 7 with 0 flagged, `check-kernel-boundary` checks 127 sources with 0 refusals, `check-doc-links` finds 775 links with 0 broken, and `git diff --check` is clean.\n\n## Not reached\n\nAdversarial inflate and delta cases in `decodePack`, CPU time decoding a 32 MiB pack (the 20-second bound is checked only before reads), the browser's View diff, a live run of the never-arrived case, and most of `docs/`.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe version binding is simple and leaves the decision to the scope. Changes requested on item 1: the unknown path needs an exit.\n\n## Security\n\nNo authority is widened, and the credential stays out of answers. Item 2 tells a caller to sign when it may not be safe, and items 4 to 6 let a hostile repository distort or break the answer. Changes requested, items 2 to 6.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe diff reader is compact. Replacing S4's local compare with the scope's answer removes code. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: changes requested, item 1.\n- Security: changes requested, items 2 to 6.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the five witnesses hold as the note describes, with witness 5 on stand-ins. Bring items 1 to 7 and one new full gate, and I'll review the diff from this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5f0c9aa4a90aeb06867ff8be935f557b07a34a8","binding":"assigned","head":"4b9f6e8907470cd607f834f3022a0a0ae8133279","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:400cc27452db7a3d602105fc686349228f8fcaec\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ca23fc67fe4892bb9ddcfaef61b23a39ccb4891f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:400cc27452db7a3d602105fc686349228f8fcaec","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:10ba8f471df3347585c598a508830c44b2b380a5, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1